%[text] # ![](text:image:2449) ![](text:image:7a3e)
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_DBB4BEDF] # Compress, Generate Code, Profile, and Deploy to Embedded Hardware
%[text] This is the final part of the workshop, and it takes the AI model the last mile - from a trained network to code running on an embedded microcontroller. In [`Exercise_1`](file:../Part_1_AI_modeling/Exercise_1.m) you trained and compared several architectures (MLP, LSTM, CNN, GRU) for battery SoC estimation; in [`Exercise_2`](file:../Part_2_AI_import/Exercise_2.m) you imported the trained PyTorch MLP into Simulink and ran it in a closed-loop system simulation. Here you follow one continuous deployment pipeline:
%[text] 1. **Compress** a network with projection to shrink its memory and computation.
%[text] 2. **Generate C code** from the model with Embedded Coder.
%[text] 3. **Inspect** the generated code.
%[text] 4. **Verify and profile** the code on the host with software-in-the-loop (SIL).
%[text] 5. **Run it on real hardware** with processor-in-the-loop (PIL) on a TI F28069M LaunchPad. \
%[text] The network we compress is the network we deploy: projection shrinks the recurrent **LSTM** - which benefits most from it - to about 1.8 KB, and that same compressed network is what runs on the microcontroller. The larger PyTorch **MLP** from Exercise 2 also generates code for the board; we keep it as a profiling baseline to show what the compression buys on-chip.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
clear
proj = currentProject;
%[text] ## 1. Open the instructions in a browser (Optional) 
%[text] We have provided an option for you to have a separate window with the instructions for this exercise for a smoother experience.
%[text] **Note:** `winopen` runs only on an installed (desktop) version of MATLAB on Windows - it is **not** supported in MATLAB Online. If you are on installed MATLAB, uncomment the line below to open the instructions in a browser. On MATLAB Online, open `Exercise_3.html` from the **Files** panel instead.
% winopen("Exercise_3.html");
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_40922387] ## 2. Load Previously Trained LSTM Network
origFileName = 'trainedNetwork.mat';
load(origFileName);
trainedNetwork.Layers %[output:20eb7a5f]
analyzeNetwork(trainedNetwork)
%[text] Note that the original LSTM network has 4.9k learnables. By default, in MATLAB, DL network learnables are stored in single-precision, floating-point format (fp32). At 4 bytes (32 bits) per learnable, this translates to a total learnables size of **~19.6 KB**. 
%[text] ![](text:image:92d0)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_23E8EF99] ## 3. Data Preparation
dataFolder = helper.getDataFolder;
trainingFile = fullfile(dataFolder,"Train", ...
    "TRAIN_LGHG2@n10degC_to_25degC_Norm_5Inputs.mat");
[predictors,response] = helper.loadData(trainingFile);

XTrain = predictors(1:10000,:);
YTrain = response(1:10000,:);
XVal = predictors(10001:50:20000,:);
YVal = response(10001:50:20000,:);

testFile = fullfile(dataFolder,"Test","01_TEST_LGHG2@n10degC_Norm_(05_Inputs).mat"); %[control:dropdown:4a1e]{"position":[39,83]}
S = load(testFile);
XTest = S.X';
YTest = S.Y;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E8868907] ## 4. Optimize the Network for the Target: Compression by Projection
%[text] Deployment is fundamentally an optimization problem: the embedded target fixes a budget for memory and compute, and the goal is to fit the most accurate network inside it. Projection is how we **optimize the network for the target** - it trades a small, controllable amount of accuracy for large reductions in learnables, memory footprint and inference time, and fine-tuning then recovers most of what was lost. The outcome is a network tuned to the F28069M's limits rather than to a workstation's. The next steps make that trade-off explicit: explore how far the network can be compressed, pick an operating point, then recover accuracy by fine-tuning.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D537DD74] ### 4.1 Understand network compression with projection
%[text] To compress a neural network, MATLAB uses a technique that involves projecting the network's layers into a smaller parameter subspace, thereby reducing the memory footprint of the model. This is done by performing principal component analysis (PCA) on the layer activations using a data set representative of the training data and applying linear projections on the layer learnable parameters. Forward passes of a projected deep neural network are typically faster when you deploy the network to embedded hardware using library-free C/C++ code generation.To learn more about the algorithm based on PCA underlying network projection, refer to [this page](https://www.mathworks.com/company/newsletters/articles/compressing-neural-networks-using-network-projection.html).
%[text] As of R2026a, the following network layers support compression with projection:
%[text] - [`convolution1dLayer`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/ref/nnet.cnn.layer.convolution1dlayer.html)
%[text] - [`convolution2dLayer`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/ref/nnet.cnn.layer.convolution2dlayer.html)
%[text] - [`fullyConnectedLayer`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/ref/nnet.cnn.layer.fullyconnectedlayer.html)
%[text] - [`lstmLayer`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/ref/nnet.cnn.layer.lstmlayer.html)
%[text] - [`gruLayer`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/deeplearning/ref/nnet.cnn.layer.grulayer.html) \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_00EE0270] ### 4.2 Explore Compression Levels
%[text] A trade-off exists between the amount of compression we apply to the network and its accuracy. In general, reducing the number of learnable parameters in a pretrained network will reduce its accuracy. Let's experiment by compressing the network with projection by a range of increasing amounts. To do this, let's iterate over 20 values of the `ExplainedVarianceGoal` in put argument to the `compressNetworkUsingProjection` function and plot the results. Then, we can decide on a worthwhile trade-off point between network size and accuracy.
%[text] The explained variance of a network details how well the space of network activations can capture the underlying features of the data. To explore different amounts of compression, you can iterate over different values of the ExplainedVarianceGoal option of the compressNetworkUsingProjection function and compare the results.
npca = neuronPCA(trainedNetwork, XTrain); % Create Neuron Principal Component Analysis (PCA) object %[output:0cbba751]
numValues = 20;
explainedVarGoal = 1 - logspace(-3,0,numValues);
explainedVariance = zeros(1,numValues);
learnablesReduction = zeros(1,numValues);
accuracy = zeros(1,numValues);

for i = 1:numel(explainedVarGoal)
    varianceGoal = explainedVarGoal(i);

    [dlnetProjected,info] = compressNetworkUsingProjection(trainedNetwork, npca, ...
        ExplainedVarianceGoal=varianceGoal, ...
        VerbosityLevel="off");

    explainedVariance(i) = info.ExplainedVariance;
    learnablesReduction(i) = info.LearnablesReduction;
    
    YPredProjected = predict(dlnetProjected, dlarray(XVal',"CT"));
    YPredProjected = extractdata(YPredProjected(:));
    accuracy(i) = sqrt(mean((YVal - YPredProjected).^2));
end
%%
%[text] Visualize the effect of the different settings of the explained variance goal.
figure %[output:2e749028]
tiledlayout("flow") %[output:2e749028]
nexttile %[output:2e749028]
plot(learnablesReduction,accuracy,'+-') %[output:2e749028]
xlabel("Learnables Reduction") %[output:2e749028]
ylabel("RMSE") %[output:2e749028]
title("Effect of Explained Variance Goal") %[output:2e749028]

nexttile %[output:2e749028]
plot(learnablesReduction,explainedVariance,'+-') %[output:2e749028]
hold on %[output:2e749028]
line([max(learnablesReduction),min(learnablesReduction)],[0.95,0.95],"Color","red","LineStyle","--") %[output:2e749028]
ylim([0 inf]) %[output:2e749028]
ylabel("Explained Variance") %[output:2e749028]
xlabel("Learnables Reduction") %[output:2e749028]
%[text] The graph shows that an increase in learnable reduction has a corresponding increase in RMSE (decrease in accuracy). A learnable reduction value of around 83\*\*%\*\* shows a good compromise between explained variance and RMSE. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7BE1822E] ### 4.3 Compress stacked-LSTM via Projection of Layers
%[text] A learnable reduction goal can be a representative parameter to choose as trade off of accuracy and magnitude of compression. Unpack projected layer is required for embedded code generation. Because the F28069M's memory budget is tight, we optimize aggressively for the target here - a 95% learnables-reduction goal - and rely on the fine-tuning step that follows to recover the accuracy.
dlnetProjected = compressNetworkUsingProjection(trainedNetwork, npca, LearnablesReductionGoal = 0.95,UnpackProjectedLayers = 1); % project network with 95% learnables reduction goal %[output:8eb6fc3e]
analyzeNetwork(dlnetProjected)
%[text] The new, projected network has 454 learnables. Still stored in `fp32`, the size now amounts to **~1.8 KB**. The prediction accuracy dropped from 0.976 to 0.839 as the cost for network compression. Next let's try to recover some lost accuracy.
%[text] ![](text:image:0a90)
%[text] 
YPredTrained = predict(trainedNetwork, XTest);
RMSETrainedNN = rmse(YTest,YPredTrained);
accuracyTrainedNN  = 1-RMSETrainedNN %[output:50b2dbe4]
YPredProjected = predict(dlnetProjected, XTest);
RMSEProjectedNN = rmse(YTest,YPredProjected);
accuracyProjectedNN  = 1-RMSEProjectedNN  %[output:58dbfe17]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FDD8C48A] ### 4.4 Fine-tune Compressed Stacked-LSTM Network
%[text] Starting with a pretrained network, the projected network equivalent can be directly computed as demonstrated above. Although a high degree of data covariance is retained, the network may need fine-tuning as accuracy can drop following the compression. This could be a result of higher order nonlinear relations between neurons in the network needing to readjust given the linear transformation or a compounding effect if many layers are projected at once. The projected model provides a good initialization for fine-tuning by further retraining. The fine-tune retraining typically takes far fewer epochs for the accuracy to plateau and can largely recover, in many cases, the accuracy of the original network.
doTraining = true; % Set to true if you would like to run fine-tuning, otherwise, the script will load a fine-tuned model from file below
if doTraining   %[output:group:278c8f6b]
    options = trainingOptions("adam", ...
        MaxEpochs = 300,...
        ExecutionEnvironment = "auto", ...
        InitialLearnRate = 1e-2, ...
        LearnRateSchedule = "piecewise",...
        LearnRateDropPeriod = 120,...
        LearnRateDropFactor = 0.1,...
        GradientThreshold = 1,...
        Shuffle = "every-epoch",...
        Verbose = false,...
        ValidationFrequency = 10, ...
        MiniBatchSize = 32, ...
        Plots = "training-progress", ...
        ValidationData = {XVal,YVal});
    
    dlnetFineTuned = trainnet(XTrain,YTrain,dlnetProjected,"mse",options); %[output:6563f73e]
else
    projectedModelName="dlnetFineTuned.mat";
    load(projectedModelName);
end %[output:group:278c8f6b]
YPredFineTuned = predict(dlnetFineTuned, XTest);
[RMSEFineTunedNN,residualsFineTunedNN] = rmse(YTest,YPredFineTuned);
accuracyFineTuned = 1 - RMSEFineTunedNN %[output:12241e7b]
figure %[output:724cc8e7]
histogram(residualsFineTunedNN); %[output:724cc8e7]
title("Residuals","RMSE: " + RMSEFineTunedNN) %[output:724cc8e7]
ylabel("Sample Count") %[output:724cc8e7]
xlabel("Y-Y_{pred}") %[output:724cc8e7]
%[text] Save the fine-tuned projected model for simulation and code generation. 
save(fullfile(helper.getCurrPartFolder("P3"), "dlnetFineTuned.mat"), "dlnetFineTuned");
%%
%[text] ## 5. (Optional) Generate C Code for Deep Learning Models from Command Line Interface
%[text] **Command-line API for code generation**
% Ensure model is loaded
model = 'SOC_model_Code_Gen';
load_system(model);
% Select Embedded Coder system target (ERT) and set code generation to produce code only (no build/executable)
set_param(model, 'SystemTargetFile', 'ert.tlc', 'GenCodeOnly', 'on');
% Generate code
slbuild(model);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6E911296] ## 6. Generate C Code for Deep Learning Models from Embedded Coder App
%[text] Open the model:
open_system("SOC_model_Code_Gen");
%[text] To generate code from the **AI subsystem** and inspect the **Static Code Metrics** Report:
%[text] **TODO:**
%[text] 1. Open the **Embedded Coder** app from the APPS Gallery.
%[text] 2. Click the **Build** button on the C CODE tab. \
%[text] ![](text:image:8b85)
%[text] ![](text:image:0400)
%[text] <u>Optional</u>: here are the configuration steps that have been set up for you for your reference. These configurations have been saved with the model.
%[text] 1. Click into the **AI** **subsystem** and apply the same settings for both LSTM and LSTM Projected subsystems. On the **Subsystem Block** pane, under **Component**, click **Atomic Subsystem**. Right-click the subsystem, and select **Block Parameters** **(subsystem)**. In the **Code Generation** tab, set **Function Packaging** to **Nonreusable function**.
%[text] 2. Open the Configuration Parameters dialog box. In the Simulink Editor, on the **Modeling** tab, click **Model Settings**.
%[text] 3. On the **Code Generation** pane, under **Target selection**, specify the system target file as `ert.tlc`.
%[text] 4. Under **Build process**, uncheck **Generate code only**
%[text] 5. On the **Code Generation** \> **Optimization** pane, set **Leverage target hardware instruction set extensions** to **None** \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_46F67580] ## 7. Inspect Generated Code
%[text] **TODO:**
%[text] 1. Click **Open Report** on the **C Code** tab of the Simulink Toolstrip. \
%[text] ![](text:image:8a21)
%[text] **TODO:**
%[text] 1. After the **Code Generation Report** window opens, in the **Code** section on the left-hand panel, click through the **Model files** to inspect the generated code. \
%[text] ![](text:image:64f8)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7BC877CC] ## 8. Profile the Generated Code on the Host (SIL)
%[text] With the code generated, check it on your computer before going near hardware. **Software-in-the-loop (SIL)** compiles the generated C code and runs it on the host, letting you confirm it behaves identically to simulation and measure how long each function takes.
%[text] **TODO:**
%[text] 1. Open the Model **Configuration Parameters** (Ctrl+E).
%[text] 2. Enable **Measure task execution time**.
%[text] 3. Make sure **Enable portable word sizes** is checked. \
%[text] ![](text:image:2b6b)
%[text] **TODO:**
%[text] 1. On the **Apps** tab, in the **Code Verification, Validation, and Test** group, open the **SIL/PIL Manager** app.
%[text] 2. On the **SIL/PIL** tab, set the mode to **SIL/PIL Simulation Only**.
%[text] 3. Under the **Prepare** section, set **SIL/PIL Mode** to `Software-in-the-loop (SIL)`.
%[text] 4. Set **Stop Time** to **100** (speeds up profiling while still running a reasonably large input set).
%[text] 5. Click **Run SIL/PIL** to run the simulation and log profiling metrics. \
%[text] ![](text:image:05ec)
%[text] **TODO:**
%[text] 1. After SIL verification completes, in the **Results** section of the **SIL/PIL** tab, click the triangle to expand the list.
%[text] 2. Select **Code Profile Analyzer** from the dropdown. \
%[text] ![](text:image:84db)
%[text] **TODO:**
%[text] 1. In the **Code Profile Analyzer** window, under the **Analysis** section, select **Function Execution**.
%[text] 2. Change **Time unit** to **Microseconds (us)**.
%[text] 3. Observe the **Average Execution Time** of the different subsystems (MLP, LSTM, Projected LSTM). \
%[text] ![](text:image:65af)
%[text] <u>Notes on interpreting the results</u>:
%[text] You might see functions of the same name showing up multiple times in the "Section" column. In this example, "MLP", "LSTM", and "Projected LSTM" appear three times. This is because there are three separate functions being called on a single component:
%[text] - **Initialize** - called exactly once for initializing the simulation
%[text] - **Step** - called at each time step as simulation continues to run
%[text] - **Terminate** - called exactly once for terminating the simulation \
%[text] **Code Profile Analyzer** shows the execution times of all three functions of a component. **Initialize** and **Terminate** functions have small execution costs, whereas the execution time for **Step** function is much more significant - this is what we focus on for the analysis, as indicated in the blue box.
%%
%[text] <u>Optional</u>: here are the configuration steps that have been set up for you for your reference. These configurations have been saved with the model.
%[text] To configure code execution profiling for SIL mode simulation:
%[text] 1. Open the **Configuration Parameters** dialog box. In the Simulink Editor, on the **Modeling** tab, click **Model Settings**.
%[text] 2. In the **Code Generation** \> **Verification** pane, under **Code execution time profiling**, select the **Measure task execution time** check box.
%[text] 3. For the function execution times, select `Coarse (referenced models and subsystems only)` from the **Measure function execution times** list. This option allows you to analyze generated function code for the main model components.
%[text] 4. In the **Workspace variable** field, specify the variable name as `executionProfile`. When you run the simulation, the software generates a variable with this name in the MATLAB base workspace. The variable, an object of type `coder.profile.ExecutionTime`, contains the execution time measurements. In the **Data Import/Export** pane, under **Save to workspace or file**, select the **Single simulation output** check box and specify the variable name as `out`. The software also creates the `executionProfile` variable in the `out` variable (`Simulink.SimulationOutput` object).
%[text] 5. In the **Code Generation** \> **Verification** pane, under **Code execution time profiling**, select `Metrics only` from the [Save options](https://www.mathworks.com/help/ecoder/ref/saveoptions.html) (Embedded Coder) list. This option helps reduce bandwidth usage for the communication channel between Simulink and the target application.
%[text] 6. In the **Hardware Implementation** pane, select the type of hardware to use to implement the system represented by the model. This example uses the default value `x86–64 (Linux64)`.
%[text] 7. Click **OK**. \
%[text] The prediction implementations that use MATLAB Function blocks require support for variable signals to be enabled for code generation. To enable code generation support:
%[text] 1. Open the Configuration Parameters dialog box. In the Simulink Editor, on the **Modeling** tab, click **Model Settings**.
%[text] 2. In the **Code Generation** \> **Interface** pane, under **Software environment**, select the **variable-size signals** check box. \
%%
%[text] ## 9. (Optional) Deploy to Embedded Hardware: TI Piccolo F28069M LaunchPad
%[text] The SIL profiling above runs the generated code on your computer - a host-based estimate. The final step is to run that same code on the **real chip** and measure its true behaviour there, using **processor-in-the-loop (PIL)**. For this we deploy the **compressed LSTM** we built above - the same projected, fine-tuned network, now small enough (~1.8 KB) to fit the device - to a TI Piccolo F28069M LaunchPad, a low-cost C28x microcontroller of the class used in production battery management systems. Open the deployment model:
load deployTestData_LSTM.mat
steps = 2000;
open_system("SOC_LSTM_Deploy_TI_F28069");
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:1717)
%[text] The model wraps only the neural network in an atomic subsystem (**LSTM\_Estimator**) - this is the unit that runs on the microcontroller. The compressed LSTM from the projection step above has just 454 learnables (about 1.8 KB in single precision) - a \>90% reduction from the original 4.9k-learnable network - so it fits easily in the F28069's 256 KB flash. A root **Inport** streams recorded LG HG2 test data (`deployTestData_LSTM.mat`) into the network through the model's External Input, so no battery, sensors or analog wiring are needed - and because the data is streamed rather than compiled into the firmware, the model builds for the board at any test length.
%%
%[text] ### 9.1 Verify Without the Board: SIL
%[text] You met SIL above as the host profiling step; here it returns as the equivalence check that must pass before running on the board.
%[text] **TODO:**
%[text] 1. With `SOC_LSTM_Deploy_TI_F28069` open, open the **SIL/PIL Manager** (on the **Apps** tab).
%[text] 2. Set **SIL/PIL Mode** to `Software-in-the-loop (SIL)`.
%[text] 3. Click **Run SIL/PIL**. \
%[text] Simulink runs the model in normal mode, then re-runs it from the generated C code in SIL mode and compares the two automatically - so the code is verified against the simulation before any hardware is involved:
%[text] ![](text:image:de02)
%[text] The generated code matches the simulation to within single-precision rounding: SIL on the host to about 2e-7, and PIL on the C28x silicon to about 1e-7 (verified live on the board).
%[text] **What the graph shows:** this is the deployed compressed LSTM's SoC estimate (blue) against the true SoC (yellow) over the **full -10 degC drive cycle** - the same test data and shape you saw running the network in [`Exercise_2`](file:../Part_2_AI_import/Exercise_2.m). The estimate follows the true SoC through every charge and discharge of the cycle. The residual noise and the RMSE of about 0.064 (at this coldest, hardest condition) reflect the aggressive \>90% compression applied earlier - the trade-off for shrinking the network to 1.8 KB; warmer cycles are smoother. The gap from the true curve is the estimation error of the compressed network itself, not a code generation artifact - the simulation, SIL and on-board PIL outputs are identical to single-precision rounding, so the curve the F28069M produces is exactly the one above.
%%
%[text] ### 9.2 Run on the Board: PIL
%[text] Running on the real chip requires the **C2000 Microcontroller Blockset** and a physical F28069M LaunchPad. If you have them, processor-in-the-loop verification follows the same workflow you used for SIL:
%[text] **Optional (requires the C2000 Microcontroller Blockset and the LaunchPad):**
%[text] 1. Install the **C2000 Microcontroller Blockset** from the Add-On Explorer and complete its hardware setup screens (this also installs the TI C2000 compiler).
%[text] 2. In **Model Settings** \> **Hardware Implementation**, set **Hardware board** to `TI Piccolo F28069M LaunchPad`.
%[text] 3. On the **Apps** tab, open the **SIL/PIL Manager**, set **SIL/PIL Mode** to `Processor-in-the-Loop (PIL)` and **System Under Test** to `Top model`, then click **Run Automated Verification**. \
%[text] In PIL mode the recorded test inputs are streamed from Simulink to the board over the USB debug link at every time step, and the prediction computed on the C28x core is sent back and compared against the normal-mode simulation automatically - the difference (lower trace in the Simulation Data Inspector) stays within single-precision rounding across the whole cycle.
%[text] ![](text:image:8176)
%[text] Because code execution profiling is already configured, the same run also reports the real execution time of the network on the target - the on-hardware equivalent of the SIL profiling above. 
%[text] ![](text:image:53bd)
%[text] Measured on the 90 MHz F28069M: about **0.76 ms per inference** (initialization 18 us) - only about **0.076% CPU** at the model's sample rate, leaving enormous headroom for typical SOC estimation rates of 1-10 Hz.
%[text] ![](text:image:28b8)
%[text] Because the input streams over the serial link rather than being compiled into the firmware, the model builds and runs PIL at any length - the profiling above comes from a full-cycle on-board run of all 39,293 inferences (see the **Calls** column). Wall-clock PIL time is dominated by per-step serial transfer over the USB debug link, not by compute: each on-chip inference is only 0.76 ms.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_LSTMVSMLP] ### 9.3 What compression bought on-chip
%[text] The same code-generation and PIL workflow applied to the larger PyTorch MLP from [`Exercise_2`](file:../Part_2_AI_import/Exercise_2.m) (9,089 learnables, ~36 KB) also runs on the board - but it costs about **3.9 ms per inference at 0.40% CPU**. Compressing the LSTM to 1.8 KB brings that down to **0.76 ms at 0.076%** - roughly **5x faster and 5x lighter on the same chip**. That on-chip saving is what the projection step earlier paid for, at the cost of the modest accuracy drop seen in the graph above.
%[text] ![](text:image:3f72)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F04983F6] Root mean squared error helper function
function [rmseValue,residuals] = rmse(y,t)
    residuals = y(:)-t(:);
    rmseValue = sqrt(mean(residuals.^2));
end
%%
